<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somali National Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1981">1981</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SNM</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> National Movement was a rebel organisation founded in 1981, drawing mainly on the Isaaq clans of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, to fight the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>. The government's bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a> in 1988 killed many civilians. After Barre's fall the movement declared the independence of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> in May 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement.jpg" alt="Somali National Movement" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dabaqabad · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Somali_National_Movement.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement was founded in April 1981 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> intellectuals, dissidents and former diplomats in Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, among them Hassan Isse Jama, Ahmed Mohamed Gulaid and Omar Elmi Dihood, with the stated aim of overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> regime. Its secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> group rejected the wish of some members based in Saudi Arabia to form an Islamic movement. Its military core came from the Fourth Brigade of the Western Somali Liberation Front, known as Afraad, whose Isaaq officers turned against the abuses of Isaaq civilians and from February 1982 moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1982 the movement moved its headquarters to Dire Dawa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, from where it raided the Maroodi Jeex and Togdheer regions. On 2 January 1983 its commandos attacked Mandera Prison near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> and freed a group of prisoners, more than 700 by the movement's reports and about a dozen by independent estimates. The regime answered with mass repression of the Isaaq. When Barre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengistu-haile-mariam">Mengistu Haile Mariam</a> made peace in 1988, the movement moved into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, and between 27 and 31 May 1988 it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>.</p><p>The army retook both cities by the end of July, and about 50,000 people were killed between March 1988 and March 1989 in its assault on the Isaaq. The movement also committed abuses: a field report by Robert Gersony found that in 1988 it attacked nine of the twelve UN-administered refugee camps in the region, killing more than 400 refugees, 60 percent of them children. By February 1991 it controlled the north-west, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Lacking a foreign sponsor, the movement relied on its Isaaq constituency, through clan collections in Saudi Arabia and the Gulf and a levy of one young man and one sheep from each household; after 1988 clan elders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-elders-somaliland">Guurti</a>, joined its central committee. A peace conference with non-Isaaq clans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> in February 1991 and the Grand Conference of the Northern Clans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a>, from 27 April to 18 May 1991, led to the declaration of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdirahman-ahmed-ali-tuur">Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur</a> as interim president. Many former members helped to form its government and constitution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Somali National Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the military regime of Siad Barre, which seized power in 1969, opposition grew among the Isaaq, the largest clan family in the north, and in 1981 exiles in London founded the <b>Somali National Movement</b> (SNM).</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebel movements, including the <b>Somali National Movement</b> in the north and the United Somali Congress in the south, closed in, and in January 1991 Barre fled Mogadishu.</blockquote><a href="/state/somali-democratic-republic">Somali Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
